\title{Renal Denervation for Resistant Hypertension:
A Randomized Sham-Controlled Trial}
\author{The SPYRAL-HTN Collaborative Group}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
\input{abstract}
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Resistant hypertension, defined as blood pressure that remains above goal
despite concurrent use of three antihypertensive agents of different
classes, carries a high risk of cardiovascular events.
Catheter-based renal denervation has been proposed as an adjunctive
therapy, but earlier trials yielded conflicting results.
\section{Methods}
We conducted a multicenter, randomized, sham-controlled trial at
twenty-four centers in five countries.
Eligible patients were randomly assigned in a two-to-one ratio to
undergo catheter-based renal denervation or a sham procedure.
The primary efficacy endpoint was the change from baseline in mean
twenty-four-hour ambulatory systolic blood pressure at six months.
